WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:05.090
With her quick wit, girl-next-door vibe, and
a last name that screams "Hollywood legacy,"

00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:07.980
big-screen glory seemed a certainty for Bridget
Fonda.

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:12.640
And from the late '80s into the '90s, Fonda's
career was the very definition of glorious.

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:14.300
Then... she disappeared.

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:18.820
Since 2002, Fonda hasn't appeared in a single
film or TV show.

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:23.220
If you're among the many fans wondering where
she went, don't worry: we dug up a few answers

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:23.720
for you.

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:25.400
Too much, too soon

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:30.480
Fonda's family name all but demanded a big-screen
breakthrough, and Fonda definitely made the

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:31.980
most of her time in the spotlight.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:38.430
Between 1987 and 1998, she consistently made
three to four films a year—more than 30

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:38.920
in all.

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:42.500
With that sort of workload it's easy to imagine
that Fonda simply burned out.

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:43.910
And who could blame her?

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:47.240
Fonda was basically working nonstop for 11
straight years.

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:49.680
We can only imagine the effect it had on her.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:51.240
The 'it girl' problem

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:56.340
With roles in the hit thriller Single White
Female and the acclaimed Cameron Crowe dramedy

00:00:56.350 --> 00:01:01.280
Singles—plus cameos in films like 1992's
Army Of Darkness—it looked like there was

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:02.860
no genre she couldn't handle.

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:07.720
And she could draw solid box-office numbers
too—Single White Female alone made nearly

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:09.660
$50 million upon release.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:13.240
With that sort of success, Fonda's "it girl"
status was unavoidable.

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:17.540
While Fonda continued to work steadily in
the years that followed, her momentum slowed

00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:22.351
during the late '90s—and as many other stars
can attest, once your career cools off, it's

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:23.540
hard to heat it up again.

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:25.000
Monkeybone

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:29.600
The crowded schedule Fonda maintained at her
peak may actually help explain some of her

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:35.010
more questionable career choices—none of
which proved quite as questionable as 2001's

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:35.620
Monkeybone.

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:40.900
Pitched as a madcap action/animation/comedy
mashup, it probably sounded like a risk worth

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:45.540
taking for Fonda; unfortunately, it proved
to be three things that Hollywood just won't

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:49.560
tolerate—expensive, difficult to market,
and almost unwatchable.

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:54.300
Monkeybone tanked at the box office, and although
she wasn't blamed for its poor performance,

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:57.580
it remains one of Fonda's last few big-screen
efforts to date.

00:01:58.060 --> 00:01:59.380
TV didn't pan out

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:04.700
Television producers pursued Fonda for years—in
fact, she actually turned down the lead role

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:08.780
on Ally McBeal in 1999 so she could focus
on making movies.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:13.660
It's tough to second guess that decision—Ally
McBeal ended quietly after five seasons, and

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:17.600
didn't exactly set its lead, Calista Flockhart,
on the path to film stardom.

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:20.700
But Fonda's own television efforts didn't
really pan out, either.

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:25.520
She took a supporting role in the Chris Isaak
Show, and appeared in the TV movies No Ordinary

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:26.980
Baby and Snow Queen.

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:31.739
The latter release premiered in December of
2002, and Fonda hasn't appeared in a film

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:33.560
or television project since.

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:35.120
Family shadow

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:39.160
We can't imagine it was an easy decision for
Fonda to walk away from acting.

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:44.160
After all, when your father is legendary actor
Peter Fonda, your grandfather is legendary

00:02:44.170 --> 00:02:49.780
actor Henry Fonda, and your aunt is legendary
actor Jane Fonda, a career in Hollywood might

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:52.400
seem like more of a birthright than a career
choice.

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:56.760
But legacies also come with expectations,
and when your family has as many heavy hitters

00:02:56.760 --> 00:03:00.200
as the Fonda clan, those expectations are
essentially tripled.

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:05.440
Though Bridget undeniably had a solid career,
it was hardly the stuff of legend—and sometimes

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:07.460
it's tough to break out of your family shadow.

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:08.880
Married life

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:13.459
The vagaries of Hollywood aside, Fonda had
another personal reason to stop looking for

00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:16.100
roles: her relationship with composer Danny
Elfman.

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:21.300
Shortly after a 2003 car crash left Fonda
with a fractured vertebrae, the actress announced

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:23.820
her engagement to the former Oingo Boingo
frontman.

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:28.799
After they married in 2003, she spent time
helping him raise his children from a previous

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:32.660
relationship, and the pair welcomed their
own son, Oliver, in 2005.

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:35.060
Fonda has maintained a low profile since.

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:36.440
Never too late

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:40.300
Who's to say that Bridget Fonda won't find
her way back to the red carpet one day?

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:44.380
Hollywood loves a good comeback, and for proof,
look no further than Bridget's own father.

00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:49.160
Peter Fonda spent the better part of the '70s,
'80s, and '90s toiling away in B-movies.

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:54.260
He didn't break back into Hollywood's good
graces until his Oscar-nominated turn in 1997's

00:03:54.260 --> 00:03:55.240
Ulee's Gold.

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:59.220
In this new golden age of television, we'd
like to think Bridget's road back would be

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:03.939
a little easier, and a challenging supporting
role on a great Netflix or Amazon series might

00:04:03.940 --> 00:04:06.180
be just the ticket to get her back in front
of cameras.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:07.480
Thanks for watching!

